# OmicFinder / Logan span-tuning experiment configuration.
# Edit RAW_ROOT (or LOGAN_FILE_LIST) before running the Logan-scale stages.

PROJECT_ROOT="/projects/logan_compression"
# Project allocation supplied by the experiment owner. df(1) on a shared filesystem
# may report filesystem free space rather than this directory quota.
# CORRECTED 2026-08-25: 4TB was the CSV's raw/uncompressed FASTA-size
# estimate (5.20 TB, actually), not a measurement of what the .zst corpus
# actually occupies on disk. `du` on the raw corpus directory itself hangs
# at this file count (see LOGAN_FILE_LIST note above), so this is a
# cross-validated estimate, not a full du: direct random sampling of .zst
# file sizes gave ~2.15-2.48 TB; independently, the raw/compressed size
# ratio (5.20 TB / 2.097x) gives ~2.48 TB. Using the upper end of that
# range (conservative for headroom math -- assumes more space is already
# spoken for).
PROJECT_QUOTA_BYTES=10000000000000
RAW_CORPUS_BYTES=2480000000000
STATIC_OTHER_BYTES=0
ECOLI_ROOT="${PROJECT_ROOT}/Ecoli"
BLE_ROOT=""

# Logan unitig data downloaded in the previous s5cmd job.
# RAW_ROOT would be /projects/logan_compression/unitigs_spans_20 -- do NOT
# point inventory_raw.py at it via --root (a bare `ls -la` on that directory
# alone was measured to hang in this project, ~7M files). Use
# LOGAN_FILE_LIST instead, which avoids ever walking that directory.
#
# Generated 2026-08-25 via the project's own kmer_spans/download_unitigs_span.py
# --generate-only (a single streaming pass over the local unitigs_with_bins.csv,
# no directory walk needed -- that CSV exists only in the local repo, not on
# the cluster, so this was run locally and just the resulting list uploaded).
# Confirmed matches the known corpus numbers exactly: 7,153,150 accessions,
# 4.73 TiB. This is the FULL span<=20 list -- selecting a scope subset from
# it (still an open decision, see kmer_spans tasks/current_task.md) is a
# separate step, not done here.
RAW_ROOT=""
LOGAN_FILE_LIST="${PROJECT_ROOT}/rsalles/span_tuning_paper/manifest/logan_span20_filelist.txt"

# Native-.zst reading (H9 in kmer_spans docs/hypotheses.md), verified
# 2026-08-26 through the real, unmodified kmhelpers profile/compose/apply/
# query pipeline against real Logan .zst files -- not just a standalone
# kmtricks test. A patched fork of kmtricks (kmtricks v1.6.0, matching the
# exact version bioconda kmtricks 1.6.0 is built from) routes GATB's
# gzopen/gzread/gzclose through zstd's own zlibWrapper, already vendored in
# this project's build but previously unused. When enabled, kmtricks/kmindex
# read .zst directly -- no materialize_manifest.sh decompression step
# needed at all; BUILD_MANIFEST becomes just MANIFEST (below).
#
# Off by default: this is a working prototype, not an upstreamed or
# widely-tested build -- only verified at small-to-medium scale (up to 394
# real files end-to-end; up to ~2.4M files for the read-only mechanism
# alone), and non-AVX2 portability is assumed (built with -march=native
# disabled) but not confirmed on a node that actually exercised the
# patched code path. Turn on deliberately, not as a default.
USE_NATIVE_ZSTD_KMTRICKS=0
NATIVE_ZSTD_KMTRICKS_BIN_DIR="${PROJECT_ROOT}/rsalles/zst_native_patch_build/kmtricks-v160-zstd-fork/bin"

# Keep experimental outputs separate from the ~2.48 TB raw corpus.
# WORK_ROOT must include rsalles/: PROJECT_ROOT alone is the shared project
# root, out of the cluster write-scope rule (writes are only authorized
# under PROJECT_ROOT/rsalles/, with an explicit separate exception for
# unitigs_spans_20/ and unitigs_spans_20_gz/, neither of which is under
# WORK_ROOT). A bare "${PROJECT_ROOT}/span_tuning_paper" here was a real,
# previously-latent bug -- every path derived from WORK_ROOT (MANIFEST_DIR,
# DESIGN_ROOT, BUILD_ROOT, RESULTS_ROOT, QUERY_ROOT, MODEL_ROOT,
# DECOMP_ROOT) would have written outside the authorized scope the first
# time any of them was used through this exact config.env for a real
# write; caught by a smoke test before that happened. LOGAN_FILE_LIST
# above already had the correct rsalles/-scoped path (hardcoded, not
# derived from WORK_ROOT), which is why this divergence went unnoticed.
WORK_ROOT="${PROJECT_ROOT}/rsalles/span_tuning_paper"
MANIFEST_DIR="${WORK_ROOT}/manifest"
# TEMPORARY, for the training-matrix fit phase only: points at a real
# N=60,000 random subsample of the full corpus (fit-small/validate-large
# strategy -- fitting the cost model doesn't need the full 7.15M-accession
# corpus, only the final anchor/validation configs do). REVERT to
# "${MANIFEST_DIR}/logan_k25.jsonl" (the full corpus) before the
# full-corpus anchor/validation builds -- do not leave this pointed at the
# subsample once the training phase completes.
MANIFEST="${MANIFEST_DIR}/logan_k25_train60k.jsonl"
# Per-accession stats CSV (seqstats_unitigs_nbseq/seqstats_unitigs_sumlen)
# that build_unitig_manifest.py joins against instead of streaming FASTA --
# same two columns create_fof.py's index_id_to_size() already uses.
# dynamodb_tigs_stats_sorted.csv, not unitigs_with_bins.csv: both have the
# needed columns (verified interchangeable -- see
# kmer_spans docs/implementation_log.md), but this one is 3 columns instead
# of 4 (no unused unitigs_log2_span), so there's no reason to prefer the
# larger file.
STATS_CSV="${MANIFEST_DIR}/dynamodb_tigs_stats_sorted.csv"
DESIGN_ROOT="${WORK_ROOT}/designs"
BUILD_ROOT="${WORK_ROOT}/builds"
# Persistent, reused-across-every-config cache of decompressed samples.
# kmtricks cannot open .zst directly (confirmed empirically -- see kmer_spans
# docs/experiment_status.md, ".zst input is NOT supported by kmtricks").
# Every candidate (base, groups) configuration builds over the SAME sample
# set -- only the grouping differs -- so this is materialized ONCE via
# scripts/materialize_manifest.sh and reused, never decompressed per-config.
# This sits on disk *alongside* the ~2.48 TB raw corpus, not instead of it
# -- budget for it explicitly (see DECOMP_ESTIMATE_BYTES below) rather than
# assuming it is free.
DECOMP_ROOT="${WORK_ROOT}/decompressed"
DECOMP_MAPPING="${MANIFEST_DIR}/decomp_mapping.tsv"
# BUILD_MANIFEST is normally produced by materialize_manifest.sh and points
# at DECOMP_ROOT copies (plus any already-.gz/plain samples, unchanged).
# check_build_manifest.py guards against stray .zst entries as a safety net
# even after materialization -- UNLESS USE_NATIVE_ZSTD_KMTRICKS=1, in which
# case there is nothing to materialize: MANIFEST's .zst paths are read
# directly by the patched kmtricks fork, so BUILD_MANIFEST is just
# MANIFEST itself and materialize_manifest.sh is skipped entirely. Every
# script consuming BUILD_MANIFEST already just reads this variable, so
# nothing downstream needs to know which mode produced it.
if [[ "${USE_NATIVE_ZSTD_KMTRICKS:-0}" == "1" ]]; then
  BUILD_MANIFEST="${MANIFEST}"
else
  BUILD_MANIFEST="${MANIFEST_DIR}/logan_k25_build.jsonl"
fi
# Measured twice on independent random subsets of the real span<=20 corpus:
# a 50-file sample gave 1.5228x (zstd -> gzip -1, 8,462,518 -> 12,886,749
# bytes) and a larger, sub-second-precision-timed 974-file sample gave
# 1.5208x (consistent with the first; per-file absolute size was NOT
# consistent between the two samples, so trust the ratio, not the
# per-file KB figures from the smaller run). Applied to the CORRECTED
# RAW_CORPUS_BYTES above (~2.48TB, not the old mistaken 4TB), that's
# ~3.78TB decompressed for the full corpus -- not 6.09TB as previously
# estimated here.
#
# This changes the additive-mode conclusion: raw (2.48TB) + decompressed
# (3.78TB) = ~6.26TB, comfortably UNDER the 10TB quota with ~3.7TB
# headroom -- the full corpus DOES fit in the default additive mode after
# this correction (previously this file said it did not, and recommended
# a ~79%-by-size subset; that was based on the wrong 4TB raw figure and
# should be treated as superseded, not as a live recommendation).
#
# REPLACE_SOURCE=1 mode remains available and would shrink the footprint
# further to just ~3.78TB, at the cost of deleting the raw .zst locally
# (s3://logan-pub is public/no-sign-request, so it's re-fetchable, not
# lost -- just not free). See materialize_manifest.py's module docstring.
DECOMP_ESTIMATE_BYTES=3779000000000

# 0 (default): decompressed copies are additive alongside the raw .zst
#   corpus -- safe for a scoped-down subset with budget slack, preserves
#   the raw data.
# 1: in-place replacement -- deletes each .zst source after its
#   decompressed copy is verified good. DESTRUCTIVE to the shared raw
#   corpus directory. Every deletion is logged to
#   ${MANIFEST_DIR}/deleted_sources.tsv. Only turn this on deliberately,
#   after deciding the trade-off is acceptable for the chosen scope.
REPLACE_SOURCE=0

# Parallel decompression workers for materialize_manifest.py. Measured
# 2026-08-25 on a real 1472-file slice of the span<=20 corpus (network
# filesystem, not CPU-bound -- do not assume linear scaling):
#   1 worker:  ~12.1 files/sec (974-file timed sample)
#   8 workers:  67.33 files/sec
#   16 workers: 79.73 files/sec (+18% over 8 workers, for 2x the workers --
#               clearly diminishing returns, consistent with an I/O-bound
#               workload against the network filesystem, not CPU)
# 8 is the chosen default: most of the achievable speedup for half the
# concurrent load on the filesystem. At 67.33 files/sec, materializing the
# full ~7.15M-accession corpus is ~29.5 hours; at 1 worker it would be
# ~6.85 days.
MATERIALIZE_WORKERS=8
RESULTS_ROOT="${WORK_ROOT}/results"
QUERY_ROOT="${WORK_ROOT}/queries"
MODEL_ROOT="${WORK_ROOT}/model"

# k=25: this project's established, consistently-used index k-mer size
# throughout create_fof.py, build_indexes/, and the wheat benchmark (the wheat
# manifest even records k: 25 explicitly) -- NOT the raw Logan unitig
# construction k (31), which is a different, unrelated parameter. k=25 also
# matches kmindex's own documented convention (every construction example in
# kmindex-src/README.md and docs/kmindex/docs/construction.md uses
# --kmer-size 25). Citation: Lemane, Teo, et al. "Indexing and real-time
# user-friendly queries in terabyte-sized complex genomic datasets with
# kmindex and ORA." Nature Computational Science 4.2 (2024): 104-109.
# Do not change this back to 31 without a deliberate decision to break
# comparability with all existing project results.
KMER_SIZE=25
DATA_TYPE="assembled"
FALSE_POSITIVE_RATE=0.25
PARTITIONS_FOR_ESTIMATE=256

# Design space. The full grid is cheap because it uses only the JSONL manifest.
BASES="1.05,1.075,1.10,1.125,1.15,1.20,1.25,1.30,1.40,1.50,1.75,2.00"
# Named GROUP_SPEC, not GROUPS: `GROUPS` is a bash-reserved builtin array
# (the current user's real Unix group IDs, e.g. `id -G`) that a plain
# assignment cannot override -- `GROUPS="1-20"` here would silently be a
# no-op, and `$GROUPS` would keep resolving to the shell's real group-ID
# list (a real, confirmed bug: it broke prepare_design_space.sh's every
# real run, passing a bogus literal group-ID number to `--groups` instead
# of the intended "1-20" range -- see kmer_spans docs/implementation_log.md
# for the diagnostic trail). Don't rename this back to GROUPS.
GROUP_SPEC="1-20"
# TRAINING_CONFIGS: no derivation was previously recorded for 16 -- it was
# just the bundle's default. Two independent, actually-applicable design
# heuristics converge on roughly this range:
#  - The model has 4 free coefficients (d,a,b,c). Fitting a first-order
#    linear model with real leave-one-out CV and bootstrap CIs (both
#    planned) wants meaningfully more points than parameters -- standard
#    DOE guidance for a first-order response-surface fit is ~2-3x the
#    parameter count for a defensible fit with residual df, i.e. ~8-12
#    here. (Looser "10-20 per predictor" rules from observational-data
#    regression are calibrated for messy, uncontrolled X and don't apply
#    to a fully-controlled, space-filling design like this one.)
#  - Treating this as a computer/simulation experiment (deterministic
#    given a config, not noisy survey data) with ~2 truly free design
#    axes (base, groups -- S and M are derived from them, not independent),
#    the commonly-cited Loeppky/Sacks/Welch "n=10d" rule of thumb for
#    space-filling designs gives ~20.
# 16 sits inside both ranges (2-3x*4=8-12 .. 10*2=20), so it's a genuinely
# reasonable choice, not just a round number -- but it was picked before
# any real per-build cost was known. Revisit once the Level-B pilot builds
# (real Logan samples, see BUILD_SPACE_FACTOR below) give real build-time
# numbers: drop toward 12 if 16 builds isn't affordable in reasonable
# wall-clock time, or raise toward 20 for tighter CIs if it's cheap.
TRAINING_CONFIGS=16

# Query workload. 1000 bp is the primary Logan-Search operating point;
# 250/500 bp are query-aware extension workloads.
#
# TRAIN_QUERY_LENGTHS and VALIDATION_QUERY_LENGTHS now match
# ALL_QUERY_LENGTHS deliberately: an already-built index is queried at all
# three lengths in the same build/query/delete cycle, since additional
# query lengths against an index already in memory/on disk are cheap --
# it's the build that's expensive. Previously only 1000bp was tested during
# training/validation, deferring 250/500bp to a separate later "extension"
# phase (below) that only covered 4 anchor configs, on the assumption that
# testing more lengths would mean rebuilding. It doesn't need to. This also
# strengthens RQ5 (does the recommended config stay stable across query
# length) with data from all training/validation configs, not just 4
# anchors. EXTENSION_QUERY_LENGTHS/the extension phase machinery is kept
# for an optional focused anchor-only re-query after the recommendation is
# frozen, but is no longer load-bearing for basic query-length coverage.
ALL_QUERY_LENGTHS="250,500,1000"
TRAIN_QUERY_LENGTHS="250,500,1000"
VALIDATION_QUERY_LENGTHS="250,500,1000"
EXTENSION_QUERY_LENGTHS="250,500,1000"
QUERIES_PER_LENGTH=5
QUERY_REPEATS=3
QUERY_THREADS=4
QUERY_PARALLEL="sub"
QUERY_THRESHOLD=0.99
QUERY_Z=6
RANDOM_SEED=20260818

# Build controls.
# 40, not 16 (the earlier default): matches kmindex build's own --threads
# default, and real, measured on this cluster (same N=5000 real pilot
# design, otherwise identical): 16 threads -> 0.2104 s/sample, 40 threads
# -> 0.1333 s/sample -- a real ~1.58x speedup, sub-linear (40/16=2.5x more
# threads), consistent with a partly I/O-bound workload. Peak build
# workspace was not meaningfully different between the two (~27.7GB vs.
# ~29.7GB at the same N=5000 scale) -- no downside found. Higher thread
# counts (e.g. 72, the full core count on several cluster nodes) were not
# tested; may have more headroom, diminishing-returns territory expected.
BUILD_THREADS=40
KEEP_BUILDS=0
# Reserve at least 1 TB of the 10 TB project quota.
MIN_FREE_BYTES=1000000000000
#
# BUILD_SPACE_FACTOR / BUILD_SPACE_PER_SAMPLE_BYTES: the storage guard in
# run_one_config.sh takes max(EST_TOTAL*BUILD_SPACE_FACTOR,
# input_samples*BUILD_SPACE_PER_SAMPLE_BYTES) + MIN_FREE_BYTES -- two
# independent real-data-grounded terms, not one.
#
# History: an initial batch of 3 pilots (294-sample manifest, varying only
# the (base,groups) design) measured peak/final ratios of 87.6x, 77.0x,
# 17.7x -- the old default of 2.0 was wrong by ~50x. But peak_build_bytes
# was nearly CONSTANT across those three (~1.7-2.0GB) regardless of final
# size, which pointed at INPUT corpus size (not output size) as the real
# driver -- confirmed by a second batch of 4 pilots that instead varied
# input sample count (300/1,500/5,000/15,000 samples, real production
# decompress-then-build mode, same design held fixed): peak_build_bytes
# tracked input count directly, ~5.25-6.38 MB/sample, STABLE across that
# 50x range, while peak/estimated_total_bytes drifted 37x->46x -- i.e. the
# output-size ratio isn't even a stable number, confirming the pure
# multiplicative-on-output model is structurally wrong, not just
# mis-calibrated.
#
# BUILD_SPACE_FACTOR=100 kept as an output-size-based floor/fallback term
# (real max observed: 46x, in the second pilot batch -- 100 keeps real
# margin, not tightened further since only one design point, base=1.1/8
# groups, was tested at varying scale).
# BUILD_SPACE_PER_SAMPLE_BYTES=8000000 (8MB/sample) is the primary,
# input-count-based term -- rounded up from the observed 5.25-6.38 MB/
# sample range for margin. Only one design point tested across scale; a
# design with many small groups relative to sample count isn't ruled out
# as needing more per-sample workspace, so don't tighten this without new
# data either.
BUILD_SPACE_FACTOR=100
BUILD_SPACE_PER_SAMPLE_BYTES=8000000

# Optional command run before the first timing repetition of each query.
# Leave empty unless GenOuest provides an approved cache-eviction mechanism.
CACHE_DROP_CMD=""
STORAGE_LABEL="network"

# Optional baseline recovered from the earlier wheat/BLE benchmark, for RQ4's
# "vs. best legacy config" comparison (PAPER_MAP.md). DECIDED to skip: the old
# wheat/BLE work only tested merge-vs-no-merge, not a real base/groups sweep,
# and the project owner judged it not comparable to the Logan data anyway.
# Deliberately left blank -- prepare_design_space.sh/fit_and_recommend.sh
# both already handle this cleanly (skip the extra baseline point/comparison
# row), so nothing else needs to change.
OLD_BLE_BASE=""
OLD_BLE_GROUPS=""

# kmhelpers installation: reuse the official v0.6.3 environment already built
# and verified (E. coli tutorial passes end-to-end, correct query results)
# under rsalles/ on 2026-08-24. This is a conda env created from the official
# v0.6.3 conda/environment.yml (bioconda kmindex 0.6.1) -- portable across
# AVX2 and non-AVX2 compute nodes, unlike sbellenous's manually-built beta.
# Do NOT rebuild from source (scripts/install_kmhelpers_063.sh's old
# from-source path is unnecessary and reintroduces the portability problem;
# see scripts/install_kmhelpers_063.sh's current contents).
KMHELPERS_REPO="/projects/logan_compression/rsalles/kmhelpers_v0.6.3_official"
KMHELPERS_ENV="${KMHELPERS_REPO}/.env"
# Poll the build directory to estimate peak on-disk workspace amplification.
DISK_SAMPLE_SECONDS=60

# Node-feature constraint for srun/sbatch. The colleague's beta kmindex
# crashes with SIGILL on non-AVX2 compute nodes; the official build we use
# here is portable, but pin AVX2 anyway as defense-in-depth and because
# heterogeneous nodes can otherwise make benchmark timings incomparable.
SLURM_CONSTRAINT="avx2"
